### The @e2e scenarios of stories 31 and 32, by hand. Start RabbitMQ and PostgreSQL (docker compose up -d),
### then Messaging first (it owns the queues), then Flight Operations, Load Planning and the simulator.
### Adjust the dates to the current week: Messaging derives the date of a booking from its day of the
### month as the nearest date to today, so a booking only lands on a flight less than two weeks away.

### Story 31: two flight schedule records -> two SSMs -> flights HJ221 (Mon, Thu) and 3V11 (Mon-Wed)

POST http://localhost:8090/api/flight-schedules
Content-Type: application/json

[
  { "periodFrom": "2026-10-12", "periodTo": "2026-10-18", "weekdays": ["MONDAY", "THURSDAY"],
    "departureTime": "06:30", "arrivalTime": "09:45", "flightNumber": "HJ221",
    "departureAirport": "BRU", "arrivalAirport": "ATH", "registration": "SXBAA" },
  { "periodFrom": "2026-10-12", "periodTo": "2026-10-14",
    "weekdays": ["MONDAY", "TUESDAY", "WEDNESDAY"],
    "departureTime": "22:15", "arrivalTime": "01:05", "flightNumber": "3V11",
    "departureAirport": "LGG", "arrivalAirport": "BRU", "registration": "OOTFA" }
]

> {%
    client.test("Two records give two SSMs", function () {
        client.assert(response.status === 202, "Response status is not 202");
        client.assert(response.body.messageIds.length === 2, "Expected two message ids");
    });
%}

### The same records again: new SSMs, but no flight is created twice (see the log of Flight Operations)

### Story 32: three booking records -> three ULD messages -> three ULDs booked on HJ221 of 12 October
